    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d748">

                <pb facs="1099" n="999" sameAs="frus:frus1934v01/pg999"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">702.0641D/11</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Chargé in the Irish Free
                        State</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Denby</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 293</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1934-05-14">May 14, 1934</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: There has been received your despatch No.
                    10 of April 17, 1934, with regard to the payment by consular officers stationed
                    in the Irish Free State of the road tax for motor vehicles and the drivers’
                    license tax.</p>
                <p>The comments contained in the despatch under acknowledgment have carefully been
                    noted. With reference to your suggestion that the American consular officers at
                    Dublin be attached to the staff of the chief of mission in order that, by reason
                    of their diplomatic status thus acquired, they might enjoy immunity from the
                    taxes in <pb facs="1100" n="1000" xml:id="pg1000"/> question, I regret to inform
                    you that such action is not considered feasible.</p>
                <p>Very truly yours,</p>
                <closer>For the Secretary of State: <lb/>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Wilbur J. Carr</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
